-- ============================================================================
-- Migration 82: entity.external_ids is an array on every database (Track PC, D2).
--
-- Migration 55 declared `external_ids` as `FLEXIBLE TYPE array DEFAULT []` with
-- `IF NOT EXISTS` — a no-op wherever the field already existed. On the
-- long-lived `staging` database it did, in a shape that appears in no migration:
--
--     DEFINE FIELD external_ids ON entity FLEXIBLE TYPE object DEFAULT {}
--
-- and all 5,500 entity rows hold `{}`. The same drift class migration 80 closed
-- for `entity_alias.match_type`, found here by D2 review: the reconciler's write,
-- `SET external_ids = ['https://…']`, fails on staging with "expected a object",
-- `update_external_ids` catches it and returns False, and a real reconcile run
-- reported every link as written while writing none. Fresh databases (every test
-- container) have the array, so no test could see it.
--
-- Measured on staging before writing this: 5,500 rows are objects, 0 of them
-- non-empty, 0 arrays, 0 NONE — converting `{}` to `[]` loses nothing.
--
-- Order matters: the definition is overwritten first, so the UPDATE's `[]` is
-- accepted by the field; the UPDATE touches only rows that do not already hold
-- an array.
-- ============================================================================

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE external_ids ON entity FLEXIBLE TYPE array DEFAULT [];
-- RETURN NONE: without it the runner receives all 5,500 rows, embeddings
-- included (D2 review).
UPDATE entity SET external_ids = [] WHERE !type::is::array(external_ids) RETURN NONE;
